<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<databaseChangeLog
        xmlns="http://www.liquibase.org/xml/ns/dbchangelog"
        xmlns:xsi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema-instance"
        xsi:schemaLocation="http://www.liquibase.org/xml/ns/dbchangelog
        http://www.liquibase.org/xml/ns/dbchangelog/dbchangelog-latest.xsd">

    <!-- SCRUM-91 D5b (defect fix, 2026-07-25): the per-MANDATE collapse.

         mandate_request_entry has NO uniqueness on mandate_ref: it is keyed
         (arrival_id, sequence). One mandate accumulates a CREATE, then AMENDs, then a
         CANCEL, each its own spine entry in its own file with its own mndt_req_id and its
         own leg replies. So mnd_ext_status (004) and mandate_effective_status (005) yield
         N rows per mandate. That is CORRECT for those two: an instruction is what the
         response legs answer, and it is the exact mirror of collections' ext_tx_status,
         where a transaction IS the entity. It is wrong for CTV, for the 1:1-live check and
         for the parity gate, which all ask "what is the state of THIS mandate".

         Collapsing by picking one entry is wrong whichever entry you pick. The FSM this
         replaces (MSR MandateStateMachine) was a sequential fold over ONE row keyed
         mandate_ref, and it carried two things no per-entry projection expresses:
         terminal states are frozen, and an active mandate is never regressed by a late or
         structural leg. Latest-entry-wins expresses neither.

         The fold is therefore reformulated as ORDER-INDEPENDENT aggregate predicates, which
         is valid because every transition in the FSM is either absorbing or monotone. -->

    <!-- Arm by arm, and why each is order-independent:

         1. TERMINATE_NOW (MD07, end customer deceased) terminates from any non-terminal
            state, so its presence on ANY instruction wins. It sits above the override arm
            for the same reason it sat above everything in the FSM: MandateStateMachine
            checks isTerminateNow() before it looks at the leg, and SUSPENDED (the only
            state an override writes today) is non-terminal, so a deceased-customer verdict
            beat it there too.
         2. An explicit mandate_override wins over everything else derived (R-09). Via
            mandate_override_pick, never the table: (mandate_ref, source) is the full
            business identity, so the table join is a row fan-out (see 005).
         3. An accepted CANCEL freezes the mandate, so its presence anywhere wins over ACCP.
            TWO shapes count, and the second was missing from the spec sketch: an accepted
            reply to a CANCEL instruction (action_code CANCEL + PBSR ACCP), AND a reply that
            carries pain.012 Sts=CANC outright, which is how Fintegrate cancels a mandate
            unilaterally (D8) and lands on the CREATE instruction's own leg. Without the
            second shape that mandate fell through every arm to PDNG.
         4. ANY leg RJCT rejects the MANDATE, whatever action it answered (Sean ruling,
            2026-07-26; this arm was action_code-scoped until then). MSR's FSM never scoped
            rejection by action_code: MandateStateMachine.finalLeg (line 48) and
            structuralLeg (line 37) both return RJCT on event.isReject() BEFORE any
            action_code is inspected, and ACCP is not terminal, so a rejected AMEND against
            a live mandate went ACCP to RJCT there too. The scoped arm was therefore a
            behaviour CHANGE dressed as a faithful port. The rule Sean set is that the
            latest Fintegrate Tx response is the true response, so a rejection is terminal
            for the mandate. The arm is absorbing (once any leg rejects, no later entry can
            un-reject), so it is still order-independent.
            The A-33 external status-code-to-FSM mapping remains an open BUILD-BLOCKING
            archaeology item. If Fintegrate later attests that some reason codes reject only
            the instruction, the extension point is mandate_reason_code.system_action, the
            same reference-data mechanism arm 1 already uses for MD07/TERMINATE_NOW, NOT
            action_code: the scope of a rejection is a property of WHY it was rejected, not
            of which action was being attempted.
            This arm also covers the MRR B1a dup_in_file loser: its mndt_req_id is NULL, it
            can never receive a leg reply, and 005 maps its MRV verdict to RJCT, so a
            mandate whose only entry is that loser reads RJCT here and is not live.
         5/6. bool_or(state IN ('ACCP','EXPIRED')) encodes "never regress an active
            mandate": once any instruction activated it, a later structural leg or a pending
            row cannot pull it back to PDNG. EXPIRED counts as activated because 005 only
            reaches EXPIRED from an activating PBSR ACCP.
            Expiry is then evaluated ONCE, against max(expiry_date), the same value this row
            reports. Testing bool_or(state = 'EXPIRED') instead (the spec sketch) reads a
            mandate as EXPIRED on the strength of a superseded entry even when a later AMEND
            extended the date, and contradicts the expiry_date the same row publishes.
            As in 005, current_date under CTV's AS OF SYSTEM TIME read evaluates at the
            SNAPSHOT (A-64). That is correct for an admissibility gate; never "fix" it into
            a written column.

         KNOWN LIMITATION, accepted and recorded (D5b): a critical-field AMEND that forces
         re-authorisation (MandateStateMachine.finalLeg: accepted AMEND with criticalAmend
         returns PDNG) is the one genuinely order-dependent transition, since it must pull an
         already-ACCP mandate back to PDNG. It is not expressible here and is out of scope:
         criticalAmend has no source column on the spine, MSR derived it in Java by diffing
         against the projection row it was about to overwrite, and nothing sets it today.

         KNOWN LIMITATION, accepted and recorded (2026-07-26, with arm 4 unscoped): arm 4
         sits ABOVE the expiry arm, so a mandate that expired BEFORE a later leg rejection
         reads RJCT here while the sequential FSM, which freezes terminal states, would have
         frozen it at EXPIRED and ignored the rejection. Both are terminal and neither is
         collectable, so the business effect is nil; only the reported code differs. It is
         recorded rather than fixed because distinguishing the two needs the very ordering
         the order-independent fold deliberately discards.

         reason follows the winning arm rather than being an arbitrary max(): the
         terminating reason first, then the override's, then a terminal leg's, then whatever
         the mandate carries. It is then forced to NULL when the computed state is ACCP
         (Sean invariant, 2026-07-26: an ACCP mandate has no reason code). Without that the
         bare max() fallback published a stale code lifted off a sibling instruction that
         never rejected anything, and a client could not tell a live mandate from one that
         failed for that reason. That is why the aggregate is wrapped in a subselect: the
         computed state has to be in scope for the reason expression to test it.
         no_retry latches across instructions, exactly as the FSM's isNoRetry latched onto
         the projection row. -->

    <!-- RE-RUN GUARD, DELIBERATELY ABSENT ON THE createView CHANGESETS BELOW.
         Every view changeset here carries replaceIfExists="true", which Liquibase renders
         as CREATE OR REPLACE VIEW, so it cannot collide with an existing view and needs no
         onFail="MARK_RAN" precondition. Measured on this database 2026-09-11, the two arms
         differing only by that attribute: replaceIfExists="true" against an EXISTING view
         exited 0, and the same statement without it exited 1 with
         'relation "..." already exists'.

         A guard here would be worse than none. MARK_RAN records a changeset as applied for
         ever, so on exactly the databases this pass exists to repair, where the object is
         present but the history row is missing, a not/viewExists guard would freeze the
         OLD view definition permanently while recording it as run. The re-runnable form
         refreshes the definition instead, which is the behaviour wanted.

         This is a decision, not an omission: if you are counting changesets against
         preconditions, these are the difference. -->

    <changeSet id="007-mandate-current-status" author="dcre" runOnChange="true">
        <createView viewName="mandate_current_status" replaceIfExists="true"><![CDATA[
            SELECT g.mandate_ref,
                   g.client,
                   g.contract_ref,
                   g.debtor_account,
                   g.creditor_account,
                   g.max_collection_amount,
                   g.start_date,
                   g.expiry_date,
                   g.state,
                   CASE WHEN g.state = 'ACCP' THEN NULL ELSE g.reason END      AS reason,
                   g.no_retry
            FROM (SELECT m.mandate_ref,
                         max(m.client)                AS client,
                         max(m.contract_ref)          AS contract_ref,
                         max(m.debtor_account)        AS debtor_account,
                         max(m.creditor_account)      AS creditor_account,
                         max(m.max_collection_amount) AS max_collection_amount,
                         max(m.start_date)            AS start_date,
                         max(m.expiry_date)           AS expiry_date,
                         CASE
                           WHEN bool_or(rc.system_action = 'TERMINATE_NOW')          THEN 'RJCT'
                           WHEN max(o.state) IS NOT NULL                             THEN max(o.state)
                           WHEN bool_or(m.state = 'CANC'
                                        OR (m.action_code = 'CANCEL' AND m.stage_rank = 4
                                            AND m.raw_status = 'ACCP'))              THEN 'CANC'
                           WHEN bool_or(m.state = 'RJCT')                            THEN 'RJCT'
                           WHEN bool_or(m.state IN ('ACCP', 'EXPIRED'))
                                AND max(m.expiry_date) IS NOT NULL
                                AND max(m.expiry_date) <> ''
                                AND to_date(max(m.expiry_date), 'YYYYMMDD') < current_date
                                                                                     THEN 'EXPIRED'
                           WHEN bool_or(m.state IN ('ACCP', 'EXPIRED'))               THEN 'ACCP'
                           ELSE 'PDNG'
                         END AS state,
                         COALESCE(max(m.reason) FILTER (WHERE rc.system_action = 'TERMINATE_NOW'),
                                  max(o.reason),
                                  max(m.reason) FILTER (WHERE m.state IN ('RJCT', 'CANC')),
                                  max(m.reason))                                     AS reason,
                         bool_or(m.no_retry)                                         AS no_retry
                  FROM mandate_effective_status m
                  LEFT JOIN mandate_override_pick o ON o.mandate_ref = m.mandate_ref
                  LEFT JOIN mandate_reason_code rc ON rc.code = m.reason
                  GROUP BY m.mandate_ref) AS g
        ]]></createView>
    </changeSet>

    <!-- V1 BASELINE (SCRUM-107, owner directive 2026-08-08): man_ctv_view is defined ONCE,
         here, over the per-mandate collapse. The guarded drop that used to precede this,
         retiring an earlier definition over the INSTRUCTION-grain view, is gone along with
         that definition: v1 never creates the wrong version, so there is nothing to swap.
         The changeset id loses its "-v3" suffix for the same reason.

         The column list and order are the contract CTV reads (D7), pinned by a test, so
         MandateProjectionDao, VerdictChain.projectionMandateVerdict and the
         dcre.ctv.mandate-source toggle need no change. -->
    <changeSet id="007-man-ctv-view" author="dcre" runOnChange="true">
        <createView viewName="man_ctv_view" replaceIfExists="true"><![CDATA[
            SELECT mandate_ref, contract_ref, creditor_account, state,
                   start_date, expiry_date, max_collection_amount
            FROM mandate_current_status
        ]]></createView>
    </changeSet>

    <!-- SELECT grant to the ctv role (R-10). THIS MARK_RAN GUARD IS LOAD-BEARING ON A V1
         DATABASE and is not the retrofit kind: it converges two environments that both
         exist from day one, not two points in a migration. A Testcontainers cluster has no
         ctv role, so the grant is skipped and the view is still created; a provisioned
         cluster (RBAC from M9 / rpt 001-rpt-roles) has the role and the grant applies. An
         unguarded GRANT fails the whole migration wherever the role is absent. -->
    <!-- LIQUIBASE-SQL-EXCEPTION: GRANT has no typed Liquibase tag; it is vendor DCL (dbms
         below), not portable DDL. Idempotent by nature: re-granting is a no-op. -->
    <changeSet id="007-man-ctv-view-grant" author="dcre" dbms="cockroachdb,postgresql">
        <preConditions onFail="MARK_RAN">
            <sqlCheck expectedResult="1">SELECT count(*) FROM pg_roles WHERE rolname = 'ctv'</sqlCheck>
        </preConditions>
        <sql>GRANT SELECT ON man_ctv_view TO ctv</sql>
        <rollback>REVOKE SELECT ON man_ctv_view FROM ctv</rollback>
    </changeSet>

</databaseChangeLog>
